1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And let me just say again, then the
subspace that has only that one point,

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
that point is zero dimension, of
course.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And the basis is empty.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
If the dimension is zero, there
shouldn't be anybody in the basis.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:26.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
So the basis of that smallest subspace
is the empty set.

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And the number of members in the empty
set is zero, so that's the dimension.

7
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Okay.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Good.

9
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:43.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Now, I have just five minutes to tell
you about, well, actually about some, some,

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:55.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
this is now this last topic of small
world graphs, and leads into a lecture about graphs.

11
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And maybe a relative of that.

12
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:06.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
But let me tell you the, in these last
minutes, the graphs that I am interested in.

13
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
It's the graph where, so what is a
graph?

14
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Better tell you that first.

15
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Okay.

16
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
What's a graph?

17
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Okay.

18
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
This isn't calculus.

19
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
We're not, I'm not thinking of like
some sine curve.

20
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
The word graph is used in a completely
different way.

21
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:37.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
It's a set of, a bunch of nodes and
edges.

22
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Ages connecting the nodes.

23
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
So I have nodes like five nodes.

24
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And edges, I'll put in some edges.

25
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
I could, could include them all.

26
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Well, let me put in a couple more.

27
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:04.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
There's a graph with five nodes and
one, two, three, four, five, six edges.

28
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:11.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And some five by six matrix is going
to tell us everything about that graph.

29
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Let me leave that matrix the next time
and tell you about the question I'm interested in.

30
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Suppose, suppose the graph, it just
doesn't have this five nodes,

31
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:33.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
but suppose every, suppose every
person in this room is a node.

32
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:41.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And suppose there's an edge between
two nodes if those two people are friends.

33
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
So have I described the graph?

34
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
It's a pretty big graph, 100 nodes.

35
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:55.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And I don't know how many edges
they're in there.

36
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
There's an edge if your friends.

37
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
So that's the graph for this class.

38
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
A similar graph you could take for the
whole country.

39
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:13.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
So 260 million nodes and edges between
friends.

40
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:26.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And the question for that graph is how
many steps does it take to get from anybody to anybody?

41
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
What two people are furthest apart?
